findings.claim:
Book of the Dead is Unity's HDRP forest demo, built from Megascans photogrammetry. For forest lighting it used:
- baked 'Occlusion Probes' for sky occlusion on foliage;
- an extra grass-occlusion system;
- Area Volumes that drive atmospheric scattering and sun;
- a custom wind shader.
A community port updated to Unity 6000.3 targets M-series Macs (macOS 15+), but publishes no performance figures.

Bevy release dates on crates.io:
- 0.19.0: 19 Jun 2026.
- 0.19.1: 13 Aug 2026.
- 0.20.0-rc.1: 15 Sep 2026.
Its examples cover:
- atmosphere and atmospheric fog;
- volumetric fog and fog volumes;
- SSAO, TAA and auto-exposure;
- PCSS soft shadows and contact shadows;
- irradiance volumes and light-probe blending;
- occlusion culling and SSR.

fidelity_ceiling:
Unity HDRP can hit the full forest look: volumetric fog and shafts, physical sky, APV sky occlusion, SSGI, PCSS, contact shadows and exposure. Book of the Dead is the precedent. But HDRP is frozen and too heavy for an M2 Air at playable rates.
Unity URP on 6.7 LTS gets close with these parts:
- APV with baked sky occlusion for the dark understorey and bright gaps;
- Surface Cache GI for warm bounce, once it ships;
- soft cascaded shadows for dappled sun near the camera;
- third-party volumetric fog;
- auto-exposure;
- a shader term driven by the LiDAR canopy height model for distant canopy shadow and visibility, standing in for Virtual Shadow Maps.
Expected result: the UE5.8 look within about 150–300 m of the camera, with softer, lower-frequency bounce than Lumen.
Flax's software DDGI is conceptually the closest to Lumen but unproven on this content.
Bevy tops out below UE5.8 on lighting: no dynamic GI on the M2, only shadows, SSAO, fog, atmosphere and baked probes.
